## 2. VERIFY THE LIVE BASELINE

Before each implementation part:

1. Read the live `reos-development` branch HEAD.
2. Read the canonical state and identify the actual current gate, current subtask and criterion statuses.
3. Read the applicable task plan, EntryPoint policy and supporting workflow instructions.
4. Inspect the exact remote file paths and current blob SHAs.
5. Check relevant commits and Actions results for the exact tested code SHA.
6. Classify each target:
   - `ADD`: the path is absent and no equivalent exists.
   - `REPLACE`: the existing file owns the responsibility and needs a demonstrated correction.
   - `VERIFY`: evidence is insufficient to decide.
   - `NO CHANGE`: the existing implementation is already correct for the scope.
   - `BLOCKED`: authorization, dependency or required evidence is missing.
7. Stop when the branch, target path, content, expected SHA or task scope differs from the verified baseline. Re-audit instead of forcing an old instruction through.

The last historical preflight is evidence only. A new working session must obtain its own current baseline.

## 3. GUARDED GITHUB WRITES

Use the write method explicitly selected by the owner for the current operation.

### When PowerShell/GitHub API is explicitly selected

- Read the exact live branch, target blob and canonical state before preparing the write.
- Build a commit whose parent is the verified current HEAD.
- Validate the complete intended file set and exact target paths before moving the branch.
- Re-read the branch immediately before the ref update.
- Use a non-force ref update.
- After updating, independently re-read the commit, the actual changed paths, every changed file and canonical state.
- Print PASS only after all required validations have completed successfully in the same fail-closed execution path.
- If any validation fails, stop. Do not execute later success/reporting commands independently.

### When the owner explicitly selects GitHub web editing and full replacement

- Open the exact existing file and confirm its complete path, including punctuation and Unicode characters.
- Replace the entire existing file only when a complete replacement has been provided or approved.
- Commit the change on `reos-development` using a truthful commit message.
- Do not create a new similarly named file or duplicate.
- After saving, inspect the committed file, branch HEAD and commit changed-file list.
- If anything differs from the intended replacement, report the actual difference and stop.

Neither method permits editing canonical state to force a workflow outcome.

## 4. VERIFY REMOTE RESULTS AND KEEP PS OUTPUT TRUTHFUL

`REOS_CONTROL_CENTER/PS Output` is diagnostic evidence only.

After each attempted write, record:

- Attempt date and scope.
- Verified base HEAD.
- Target file paths and expected original blob SHAs.
- Actual error message, if any.
- Whether the branch reference moved.
- Actual commit SHA and changed-file list, if a commit exists.
- Independent remote read-back result for every intended file.
- Canonical state blob SHA and current task after the attempt.
- CI URL and exact tested SHA, if a relevant workflow ran.
- Next permitted action.

Never report a PASS after an earlier validation exception. Do not erase or misrepresent a failed attempt. When a prior PASS is found to contradict actual changed paths or read-back evidence, explicitly mark that result INVALID.

A proposed Git object is not proof that the branch was updated. A commit object is not proof that the intended paths were changed. A successful ref update is not proof that all intended file contents were read back correctly.

The verified result is the live repository read-back, not the terminal's last printed line.

